How you'd actually use them
Step 1 — open prepare_frames.py, put your unzipped folder name in the marked box, run python prepare_frames.py. It samples evenly across the whole clip, resizes, compresses, renames with zero-padding, then prints the exact settings to paste into the JS.

Step 2 — add this to any page:


<section id="hero">
  <h1>Your headline</h1>
  <p>Your supporting line.</p>
</section>
<script src="hero-scroll.js"></script>
That's it. The script builds the canvas, writes its own CSS, and keeps whatever you put inside #hero as the overlaid content.

Three things that make these agent-proof
count: 'auto' — you don't have to know how many frames you have. It doubles upward until a frame is missing, then binary-searches the gap: about 16 requests once, instead of 80 guesses. An agent that forgets to update the count still gets a working hero.

It refuses clearly instead of failing silently. No #hero? It logs exactly what markup to add. No frames? It logs the exact path it looked for. Silent nothing is the failure mode that wastes the most time.

Every bottleneck guard from the guide is already in there — requestAnimationFrame coalescing, passive listeners, repeat-index skipping, canvas sized once, hidden-hero guard, six-at-a-time loading, prefers-reduced-motion collapsing it to a single still. An agent copying this file gets those for free rather than needing to know about them.

The Python also deletes previous output by exact filename only, never by wildcard — the same guard I use in this project, because a wildcard delete in a folder you don't fully own is how you lose files that weren't yours.